.TH UPAS-FETCH 1
.SH NAME
upas/fetch \- fetch mail from an IMAP server over TLS
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B upas/fetch
[
.B -s
.I server
] [
.B -a
.I dialip
] [
.B -p
.I port
] [
.B -u
.I user
] [
.B -P
.I passfile
] [
.B -n
.I n
] [
.B -b
] [
.B -o
.I mbox
]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Upas/fetch
connects to an IMAP4 server over
.IR tls12 (2)
and, given credentials, prints recent messages from the inbox.
It defaults to Gmail
.RB ( imap.gmail.com :993).
.PP
With no
.B -u
it only probes the transport: it completes the TLS handshake, reads the
greeting, prints the server
.B CAPABILITY
and exits.
This confirms reachability and certificate verification without any
credentials.
.PP
With
.B -u
.I user
and
.B -P
.I passfile
it logs in with the password read from
.I passfile
(for Gmail, an
.I "app password"
\(em an ordinary Gmail password will be refused), selects
.BR INBOX ,
and prints the most recent
.I n
messages (default 5).
By default only the
.BR From ,
.B Subject
and
.B Date
headers are printed;
.B -b
prints whole messages.
.B -o
.I mbox
instead writes the fetched messages (whole) to
.I mbox
in Unix mbox format, which
.IR upasfs (4)
can then serve as a file system \(em so a Gmail inbox becomes browsable
with
.IR ls (1)
and
.IR cat (1):
.EX

	upas/fetch -u me@gmail.com -P pass -a 142.251.163.108 -o inbox.mbox
	upas/fs inbox.mbox

.EE
.PP
A hosted
.I emu
usually has no
.B /net/cs
to resolve names, so
.B -a
gives the server's already-resolved address to dial while the
certificate is still verified for
.I server
(the
.B -s
name).
Bind
.B "#I"
onto
.B /net
first so the IP stack is present.
.SH EXAMPLES
Probe Gmail's IMAP endpoint (no account needed):
.EX

	bind -a '#I' /net
	upas/fetch -s imap.gmail.com -a 142.251.163.108

.EE
Read the five most recent inbox headers with an app password:
.EX

	echo abcd\ efgh\ ijkl\ mnop >/tmp/gpass
	upas/fetch -u me@gmail.com -P /tmp/gpass -a 142.251.163.108

.EE
.SH SOURCE
.B /appl/cmd/upas/fetch.b
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR imap4 (2),
.IR tls12 (2)
.SH BUGS
Names are not resolved inside
.IR emu ;
supply
.B -a
with an address looked up outside.
Authentication is
.B LOGIN
only (an app password), not XOAUTH2, so a Gmail account with two-factor
authentication needs an app password.
